# The route table: which container answers which path. Imported by both front doors — Caddyfile
# (owns TLS) and Caddyfile.external-tls (behind a terminator that does) — so the paths exist once.
# Routes only: a response header set here would be sent by the plain-HTTP door too, which is
# why Strict-Transport-Security lives in Caddyfile's TLS site block and not in this file.

# NO path stripping: the API canonicalizes one leading `/api` off itself, so `/api/sync` here and
# `/sync` from a native client hash identically for idempotency. A strip would double-strip
# `/api/api/…`. `/internal/*` is the shared-secret alert intake an operator's monitor calls — 404
# until TF_ALERT_SECRET arms it, and nothing in the product's own UI ever uses it.
#
# A CONTAINER SWAP IS HELD FOR READS, NOT ANSWERED 502. While `docker compose up -d` replaces api
# or web the name stops answering for a few seconds, so a GET, HEAD or OPTIONS is re-dialled every
# 250ms for up to 10s and only then answered 502. Every other method has no window and fails fast:
# a held POST finds its body spent by the failed first attempt (measured: held 3.5 s, then 502), and
# nothing that reached the container is ever sent twice.
#
# THE VISITOR'S ADDRESS IS THIS PROXY'S DECISION. Both api blocks replace X-Forwarded-For with the
# one address Caddy settled on ({client_ip}: its peer, or what a trusted terminator named) and name
# that peer in X-Ohmail-Proxy-Peer, so the api, which believes this proxy alone, can say when the
# two are one. Spelled in both blocks: a snippet cannot be defined inside an imported file.
@replayable method GET HEAD OPTIONS
@api path /api /api/* /auth /auth/* /events /health /hello /pair /pair/* /internal /internal/*
handle @api {
	reverse_proxy @replayable api:8080 {
		lb_try_duration 10s
		lb_try_interval 250ms
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
		header_up X-Ohmail-Proxy-Peer {http.request.remote.host}
	}
	reverse_proxy api:8080 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
		header_up X-Ohmail-Proxy-Peer {http.request.remote.host}
	}
}

# The staging bucket's browser door. Uploads are presigned against THIS origin, so the path carries
# the bucket name (track S3_BUCKET) and the Host must stay as sent — SigV4 signs it, and MinIO
# recomputes over what it receives. Safe only because the bucket is PRIVATE: unsigned requests 403.
# Never attach an anonymous policy; the boot check probes an unsigned GET and PUT.
@staging path /{$S3_BUCKET:ohmail-staging}/*
handle @staging {
	reverse_proxy minio:9000
}

# Everything else is the web application, held across a swap the same way.
handle {
	reverse_proxy @replayable web:3000 {
		lb_try_duration 10s
		lb_try_interval 250ms
	}
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}
